(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-cms-exam-3-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 3 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 34-year-old woman had a cold that improved over five days, then abruptly worsened on day seven with fever, unilateral cheek pain and purulent discharge. What does this pattern represent?","choices":["A nasal foreign body","A normal viral course that needs no intervention","Allergic rhinitis triggered by the viral illness","Double worsening, which suggests bacterial superinfection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A viral rhinosinusitis follows a single curve: worse, then steadily better. A second deterioration after initial improvement means something new has happened, and bacterial superinfection is the usual explanation. Double worsening is one of the named criteria, alongside symptoms lasting ten days or more and unilateral facial or tooth pain. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 4-year-old is brought in with a few hours of high fever, drooling, refusal to swallow and a muffled voice. He is sitting upright and leaning forward with his chin thrust out, and appears distressed and anxious. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Do not examine the throat; secure the airway with the appropriate team","Examine the pharynx with a tongue depressor to confirm the diagnosis","Give oral antibiotics and arrange review in the morning","Obtain a lateral neck radiograph before any intervention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The four Ds and the tripod position describe supraglottitis, and the teaching is explicit that once it is suspected, examinations that increase the child's anxiety, including intraoral examination and venipuncture, may precipitate complete airway obstruction. The priority is a controlled airway in theatre with anaesthetic and surgical teams present. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 68-year-old with a 50 pack-year smoking history has four months of worsening dysphagia, occasional blood-streaked sputum and intermittent right ear pain on swallowing. Stridor is audible over the trachea. Nasopharyngoscopy shows a mass replacing the right true vocal cord. Which symptom would be expected?","choices":["Fever and retropharyngeal oedema","Hoarseness","Tonsillar exudates","Weight gain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A mass replacing the true vocal cord prevents that cord vibrating and apposing normally, and voice production depends on both cords meeting and vibrating together. Hoarseness is therefore the expected and usually earliest symptom of a glottic tumour, and the referred ear pain reflects shared vagal innervation between the larynx and the ear. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 144"},{"q":"A 58-year-old has reduced hearing in the left ear. Audiometry shows elevated bone conduction thresholds on the left together with an air-bone gap on the same side. There is a history of both chronic ear infections and long-standing noise exposure at work. What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["A functional hearing loss","A mixed hearing loss","A pure conductive loss","A pure sensorineural loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An air-bone gap means sound is being obstructed before it reaches the cochlea, which is conductive. Elevated bone conduction means the cochlea itself is not performing normally, which is sensorineural. Having both together defines a mixed loss, and it matters because correcting the conductive component will not restore hearing to normal. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 62-year-old smoker has a firm 3 cm node in the posterior triangle. Aspiration confirms squamous cell carcinoma and no primary is visible in the mouth or throat. The team notes which nodal level is involved before planning the search. Why does the level matter?","choices":["The level determines the histological type of the tumour","The level determines whether antibiotics are needed","The level has no bearing on management","The level predicts where the primary is likely to be"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each region of the head and neck drains to a predictable nodal group, so the level of the involved node narrows the search for the primary. That is the practical application of the drainage map: jugulodigastric nodes point to the tonsil, juguloomohyoid to the tongue, and submandibular nodes to the face, sinuses and mouth. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 29-year-old has had nasal congestion, purulent discharge and facial pain for eleven days. The illness improved around day five and then worsened again, with a new fever and pain over the left maxilla. The clinician is deciding whether this has become bacterial. Which features shift it toward a bacterial cause?","choices":["Facial pressure and nasal congestion","Fever at any point during the illness","Purulent green or yellow discharge","Ten days or more, double worsening, or unilateral pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These are the named criteria, and each captures something a virus does not usually do: viral illness improves within about ten days, follows a single curve rather than relapsing, and produces bilateral symptoms rather than pain localised to one sinus or the upper teeth beneath it. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 3-year-old with recurrent respiratory papillomatosis is deteriorating. A clinician suggests tracheostomy to secure the airway. What should the patient be told?","choices":["Avoid tracheostomy, because it creates another site the papillomas favour","Give intralesional cidofovir as first-line therapy","Give systemic antivirals to cure the human papillomavirus infection","Perform tracheostomy, as airway protection outweighs other considerations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Papillomas have an affinity for squamociliary junctions, the boundaries where ciliated respiratory epithelium meets squamous epithelium. A tracheostomy creates a new such junction, giving the disease a fresh site to seed and spreading it distally into the trachea, which is why it is specifically avoided. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 6-week-old infant has a firm, painless, discrete mass within the right sternocleidomastoid muscle and holds his head tilted to that side. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Fine needle aspiration to exclude malignancy","Immediate surgical excision","Physical therapy, as most resolve","Ultrasound followed by observation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This lesion is associated with congenital torticollis and follows a predictable course: it enlarges for two to three months, then regresses over four to eight months, and about 80 per cent resolve on their own. The purpose of physical therapy is to prevent a fixed restrictive torticollis while that natural resolution takes place. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 24-year-old reports total deafness in one ear following a minor workplace incident, but startles to a loud unexpected sound on that side and gives inconsistent audiometry. The clinician needs an objective test that does not depend on the patient's cooperation. Which investigation is most appropriate?","choices":["Auditory brainstem response testing","Repeat pure tone audiometry","Tympanometry","Weber and Rinne testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Brainstem response recording measures the electrical activity generated along the auditory pathway in response to sound, so it produces a threshold estimate without the patient having to report anything. That makes it the way to resolve a discrepancy between claimed and demonstrated hearing. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has used a topical decongestant spray daily for six weeks and now has worse congestion than when he started. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A nasal polyp obstructing the airway","Development of chronic bacterial sinusitis","Progression of an underlying allergic rhinitis","Rebound congestion from prolonged topical decongestant use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Topical alpha agonists constrict the nasal vessels, but with sustained use the receptors downregulate and the vessels dilate more each time the drug wears off. The patient then uses it more often, and the congestion worsens. That is why these sprays carry a three-to-five-day limit. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man has had three peritonsillar abscesses and multiple episodes of tonsillitis over two years. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Long-term prophylactic antibiotics","Repeated needle aspiration each time it recurs","Tonsillectomy during the current acute infection in all cases","Tonsillectomy, usually after the acute infection resolves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent tonsillitis with recurrent peritonsillar abscesses is the indication for tonsillectomy, and most surgeons prefer to operate once the acute inflammation has settled, because the tissue planes are cleaner and bleeding is less. Quinsy tonsillectomy during infection is reserved for younger children, unresponsive cases or severe airway compromise. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 9-year-old had a sore throat with fever three weeks ago that was not treated. The child now has migratory joint pains, a new murmur and a rash. The parent asks how a throat infection could have injured the heart. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Antibodies against the streptococcus cross-react with heart muscle","Streptococcal toxin directly poisons the myocardium","The bacteria spread through the bloodstream to the heart valves","The fever itself damages the myocardium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The organism shares antigenic determinants with human tissue, so the antibody response mounted against it also binds cardiac muscle, producing endocarditis, myocarditis or pericarditis. That is why the damage appears two to three weeks after the sore throat rather than during it, and why eradicating the organism early prevents it. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man strains heavily while weightlifting and hears an audible pop in the right ear, followed immediately by hearing loss and vertigo. Symptoms worsen with straining and coughing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acoustic trauma","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Meniere disease","Perilymphatic fistula"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A sudden rise in intracranial or middle ear pressure can rupture the round or oval window membrane, letting perilymph leak from the inner ear. The audible pop marks the rupture, and because the leak is pressure-dependent the symptoms characteristically worsen with straining, coughing or lifting. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 28-year-old at 24 weeks' gestation attends for routine antenatal care. She completed her childhood immunisations and had Tdap during her previous pregnancy four years ago. She asks whether she needs it again. When should Tdap be given?","choices":["Between 27 and 36 weeks of every pregnancy","Immediately after delivery","In the first trimester","Once only, before the first pregnancy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The recommendation is Tdap in each pregnancy between 27 and 36 weeks. Vaccinating in that window maximises transplacental antibody transfer, protecting the newborn during the months before the infant's own immunisation schedule has taken effect. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 114"},{"q":"A 20-year-old has a three-day sore throat with fever. On examination the palatine tonsils are enlarged and exudative and the surrounding pharyngeal wall is also erythematous. The clinician is choosing the correct term for the documentation. How are pharyngitis, tonsillitis and pharyngotonsillitis distinguished?","choices":["Pharyngitis is always viral and tonsillitis always bacterial","The distinction depends on the duration of symptoms","Tonsillitis if the tonsils are affected, pharyngitis if the throat is, both if both","Tonsillitis refers to the adenoids and pharyngitis to the palatine tonsils"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The three terms describe which structure is inflamed rather than three different diseases, and the same organisms cause all of them. Naming them separately matters mainly because tonsillar involvement raises the possibility of complications such as peritonsillar abscess. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man claims complete bilateral deafness following a workplace dispute. He responds appropriately when spoken to at conversational volume while facing away, his voice is of normal loudness, and audiometry is inconsistent between repeat tests. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bilateral cerumen impaction","Bilateral sudden sensorineural hearing loss","Functional (non-organic) hearing loss","Ototoxicity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Genuine profound bilateral loss removes the auditory feedback people use to regulate their own volume, so the voice becomes loud; a normal voice argues the hearing is intact. Responding to conversational speech when not lip-reading, together with inconsistent audiograms, points to a non-organic cause rather than cochlear damage. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy is found to have multiple grey, glistening masses in both nasal cavities, with chronic congestion and reduced sense of smell. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Prescribe an intranasal steroid and review in three months","Refer for polypectomy","Start antihistamines for presumed allergic rhinitis","Test for cystic fibrosis with a sweat chloride test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nasal polyps are distinctly unusual in children, and between 5 and 44 per cent of children with cystic fibrosis have them. Finding multiple polyps in a child is therefore an indication to look for the underlying systemic disease rather than simply to treat the polyps, and asthma should be assessed at the same time. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man with a displaced nasal fracture presents four days after injury with marked swelling. He asks when it can be straightened. What should the patient be told?","choices":["After three months, once healing is complete","Displaced nasal fractures never require reduction","Immediately, before any swelling develops","Once the swelling settles, within about two weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Marked swelling obscures the underlying bony position, so reduction attempted immediately risks an inaccurate result. Waiting a few days lets the oedema settle, but the bones begin to unite after roughly two weeks, so there is a window in which reduction is both accurate and still possible. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 12-year-old has a midline submental mass that is non-tender and mobile. It does not move when he protrudes his tongue. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Branchial cleft cyst","Dermoid cyst","Submental lymph node","Thyroglossal duct cyst"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both dermoid and thyroglossal duct cysts are midline, which is why they are the pair that must be distinguished. The discriminator is the hyoid attachment: a thyroglossal duct cyst rises with tongue protrusion, and a dermoid, arising from epithelium entrapped during embryogenesis, has no such connection and stays still. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a positive right Dix-Hallpike test asks how her vertigo will be treated. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A low-salt diet","Long-term vestibular suppressant medication","Surgical labyrinthectomy","The Epley manoeuvre"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The vertigo is caused by otoconia that have escaped the utricle into a semicircular canal, where they move with gravity and stimulate the canal inappropriately. The Epley is a sequence of head positions that rolls those particles back out of the canal into the vestibule, treating the mechanical cause rather than suppressing the symptom. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 25-year-old was struck on the nose three weeks ago and was seen at the time but not examined internally. The patient now has persistent nasal obstruction and has noticed the bridge of the nose has sunk. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A displaced nasal fracture that was missed","A septal abscess requiring drainage","Post-traumatic swelling that will resolve","Saddle nose deformity from cartilage necrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The septal cartilage has no blood supply of its own and depends entirely on the overlying perichondrium. A haematoma strips the perichondrium away, the cartilage undergoes avascular necrosis, and the dorsum loses its structural support and collapses. That is why drainage is urgent rather than elective. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman develops a firm, rubbery, progressively enlarging nodule at the site of an ear piercing six months earlier. It extends beyond the original wound margin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Epidermoid cyst","Hypertrophic scar","Keloid","Perichondritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A keloid is an overgrowth of collagen in response to skin injury, and the defining feature is that it extends BEYOND the boundaries of the original wound. Ear piercing is the classic trigger on the auricle, and the distinction from a hypertrophic scar, which stays within the wound, matters because keloids recur readily after excision. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 55-year-old smoker has a firm, non-tender 2.5 cm cervical node. There has been no recent infection, and the patient thinks it has been there about a month. The clinician is deciding whether the duration alone warrants investigation for malignancy. What duration should prompt concern?","choices":["Duration is not part of the assessment","More than 48 hours","More than six months","More than two weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Duration over two weeks appears on the malignancy red-flag list alongside size over 1.5 centimetres, firmness with immobility, absence of an infectious origin, age over 40, tobacco and alcohol use, and ulceration. Reactive nodes regress within one to two weeks, so persistence beyond that is the point at which the prior probability shifts. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with nasal polyps had endoscopic sinus surgery two years ago and his symptoms have returned. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["He has developed a new diagnosis such as malignancy","Polyps do not recur, so this must be scar tissue","Recurrence is expected after surgery","The original surgery must have been incomplete"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polyps arise from ongoing mucosal inflammation, and removing them does not remove the process that produced them. Surgery provides only temporary relief, which is why medical treatment of the underlying inflammation continues afterwards and why patients should be told to expect this. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 6-month-old has a red, soft, compressible mass on the neck that has grown since birth and becomes larger and more prominent when he cries. He is feeding well, and the airway is not compromised. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Observation","Start propranolol now","Surgical excision","Systemic corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Haemangiomas grow rapidly through the first year and then begin to involute at 18 to 24 months, and roughly 90 per cent resolve without any therapy. Enlargement with crying reflects venous engorgement in a vascular malformation rather than progression, so observation is appropriate when there is no functional compromise. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 33-year-old has had nasal congestion, facial pressure and purulent discharge for six days and asks for a scan to find out whether antibiotics are needed. The clinician explains that imaging would not answer that question. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Both fill the sinuses, so imaging cannot separate them","Computed tomography is too insensitive to show sinus fluid","Contrast is required and is contraindicated in sinusitis","The radiation dose makes it unsafe in any circumstance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mucosal thickening and fluid levels appear in ordinary viral rhinosinusitis as well as in bacterial infection, and can even be seen in asymptomatic people. Imaging therefore answers a question nobody is asking, and the distinction remains clinical, based on duration, double worsening and unilateral pain. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 60-year-old has a firm 3 cm cervical node and fine needle aspiration is being performed under ultrasound guidance. The clinician takes care to obtain enough material to avoid a non-diagnostic result and a repeat visit. How many passes are needed?","choices":["A minimum of four separate passes","One pass, provided material is obtained","Ten passes, to guarantee adequacy","Two passes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A single pass samples one small volume and risks missing focal disease within a heterogeneous node, so the standard is a minimum of four separate needle passes for collection. The material is used for cytology, Gram stain and culture, which is why adequacy of sampling determines whether the result can be trusted. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 43-year-old has a Weber test that lateralises to the left ear. The Rinne test has not yet been performed, and otoscopy of both ears is normal. Two explanations remain possible. What are the two explanations?","choices":["A left conductive loss or a right sensorineural loss","A left sensorineural loss or a right conductive loss","Only a left conductive loss","Only a right sensorineural loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Weber lateralises toward a conductive loss, because that ear is shielded from ambient noise and picks up the bone-conducted tone more clearly. It lateralises away from a sensorineural loss, because that cochlea processes the tone poorly. Both therefore point the sound to the left, and only Rinne separates them. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 2-year-old girl has a soft, doughy, non-tender, compressible mass in the posterior triangle of the neck. When a light is held against it, the mass transilluminates. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Branchial cleft cyst","Haemangioma","Lymphangioma","Teratoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A lymphangioma is a congenital malformation in which lymph spaces fail to connect to the rest of the lymphatic system, so it fills with clear lymph. Clear fluid is what allows light to pass through, which is why positive transillumination is its distinguishing bedside sign, and why the mass feels soft, doughy and compressible. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 6-year-old with mild acute otitis media, no severe pain and reliable parents is considered for observation rather than immediate antibiotics. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Antibiotics are mandatory for every case of acute otitis media","Observation is appropriate regardless of the child's age","Observation is reasonable with a plan to treat if not improving","Topical antibiotic drops can be used while observing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Many episodes resolve without antibiotics, and watchful waiting reduces unnecessary prescribing and side effects. It depends on three conditions being met: an older child, mild disease, and reliable follow-up so that antibiotics can be started promptly if there is no improvement in 48 to 72 hours. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 4-year-old has five days of high fever, bilateral conjunctival injection, a strawberry tongue, cracked lips, a rash and a large unilateral cervical node. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Infectious mononucleosis","Kawasaki disease","Reactive viral lymphadenopathy","Suppurative bacterial lymphadenitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Kawasaki is one of the non-infectious inflammatory causes of neck masses, alongside Sjogren syndrome, sarcoidosis and IgG4-related disease. The cervical node is one criterion among several, and recognising the constellation matters because untreated Kawasaki causes coronary artery aneurysms. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with allergic rhinitis is prescribed an intranasal corticosteroid. She asks how to use it so that it works and does not damage her nose. What instruction should she be given?","choices":["Aim the spray at the septum for even distribution","Aim the spray laterally, away from the septum","Sniff hard immediately after spraying","Use it only on days when symptoms are severe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Directing the spray at the septum delivers the drug repeatedly to the thin mucosa over the cartilage, and chronic vasoconstriction there is one of the named causes of septal perforation. Aiming laterally toward the turbinate treats the tissue that is actually swollen and keeps the drug off the septum. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 19-year-old wrestler is seen four hours after a match with a swollen, tense, purple left auricle. The normal folds and hollows of the ear are no longer visible. He has no hearing complaint and the canal and drum are normal. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Drainage and a pressure dressing","Ice and observation for 48 hours","Needle aspiration without a dressing","Oral antibiotics alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood collecting between the perichondrium and the cartilage strips the cartilage of its blood supply, because auricular cartilage has no vessels of its own and depends entirely on the perichondrium. Left in place the cartilage necroses and heals as the fibrotic mass called cauliflower ear, so the collection is drained early and a pressure dressing stops it reaccumulating. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 60-year-old has gradual bilateral hearing loss. On the right, air conduction is heard longer than bone conduction, which is the same pattern found in the examiner's own normal ear. A student asks why this test alone cannot diagnose a sensorineural loss. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["A sensorineural ear gives the same result as a normal one","Rinne cannot be performed accurately in sensorineural loss","Rinne only tests bone conduction","Sensorineural loss reverses the Rinne result"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rinne compares air against bone within a single ear. In sensorineural loss both pathways are reduced by the same cochlear problem, so air still beats bone exactly as in a normal ear. The test returns a normal pattern, and only Weber, which compares the two ears, reveals the asymmetry. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 61-year-old patient has a one-week history of intermittent room-spinning dizziness. Episodes last under a minute, occur a few times daily, and are triggered by turning to the right in bed. There are no auditory changes and no neurological deficits. Dix-Hallpike with the right ear down provokes brief, fatigable nystagmus. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Labyrinthitis","Meniere disease","Positional vertigo","Vestibular schwannoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Otoconia displaced into a semicircular canal move with head position and stimulate the canal until they settle, which is why the vertigo lasts seconds rather than hours and why it fatigues on repeated testing. The absence of hearing change is what separates it from the inner ear syndromes that involve the cochlea, and a positive Dix-Hallpike confirms it. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl had acute otitis media six weeks ago, treated with antibiotics. Her fever and pain resolved but her parents report she still turns the television up loudly. Otoscopy shows an amber, non-bulging tympanic membrane with an air-fluid level and reduced mobility. She is afebrile and not in pain. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A further course of oral antibiotics","Immediate myringotomy with tube placement","Observation","Oral corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Once the acute infection has been treated the sterile fluid often takes weeks to months to clear as the eustachian tube recovers. An amber non-bulging drum in an afebrile, painless child is a resolving effusion rather than persistent infection, so the appropriate action is to document the hearing and let it clear, reserving intervention for persistence or developmental concern. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 5-year-old with acute otitis media has completed high-dose amoxicillin but remains febrile with a bulging drum after 72 hours. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Add topical antibiotic drops","Change to amoxicillin-clavulanate","Continue the same course for a further week","Stop antibiotics and observe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Failure after 72 hours of adequate first-line therapy suggests a beta-lactamase producing organism, typically Haemophilus influenzae or Moraxella catarrhalis, which amoxicillin alone does not cover. Adding clavulanate restores activity against those organisms without abandoning the pneumococcal coverage that amoxicillin provides. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with otitis externa has spreading erythema over the pinna and onto the face, with fever and regional lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Add systemic antibiotics","Continue topical therapy alone at a higher frequency","Irrigate the canal to clear debris","Stop all antibiotics and use a topical steroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical therapy is first line precisely because the disease is confined to canal skin, and drops reach that surface directly. Once the infection has extended into surrounding soft tissue, producing cellulitis with fever and regional nodes, it is beyond the reach of a topical agent and needs a systemic one. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is admitted for stapedectomy to treat otosclerosis. He has had progressive hearing loss in the right ear over several years. His mother had the same condition in her forties and had a successful operation. Weber and Rinne testing is performed before surgery. Which finding would be expected on tuning fork testing?","choices":["Air conduction exceeds bone conduction on the affected side","Bone conduction equals air conduction on the affected side","Bone conduction exceeds air conduction on the affected side","Sound lateralises to the unaffected ear on Weber testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Otosclerosis lays down abnormal bone that fixes the stapes footplate in the oval window, so the ossicular chain can no longer drive the cochlear fluid. The cochlea itself is normal, so bone-conducted sound still reaches it perfectly well while air-conducted sound is blocked at the footplate. That is the definition of a conductive loss, and a reversed Rinne is how it is demonstrated. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man using an inhaled corticosteroid for asthma has white patches on his buccal mucosa and tongue. When the clinician wipes one with a tongue depressor it comes away, leaving an erythematous base. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Leukoedema","Oral candidiasis","Oral leukoplakia","Oral lichen planus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The single most useful bedside manoeuvre in a white oral lesion is to try to wipe it off. Candida forms a pseudomembrane that is removable, revealing inflamed mucosa underneath. Inhaled corticosteroid deposited in the mouth suppresses local immunity and is a common cause, which is why patients are told to rinse after using the inhaler. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with symmetrical high-frequency sensorineural loss asks why she can hear her grandson's voice but cannot understand his words. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Consonants are high-frequency sounds and carry most of the meaning","Her hearing loss is worse for low frequencies","She has a conductive component reducing overall volume","The problem is central rather than cochlear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vowels are low-frequency and loud, so they remain audible and give the impression that speech is being heard. Consonants sit at high frequencies and are quieter, and they are what distinguish one word from another. Losing the high frequencies therefore removes the information while leaving the sound. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with a known cholesteatoma develops vertigo and a facial droop involving the forehead on the same side. What does this combination of findings indicate?","choices":["She has developed a separate Bell palsy","The cholesteatoma is eroding into the labyrinth and facial nerve","The vertigo indicates benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","This represents Ramsay Hunt syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A cholesteatoma expands by shedding keratin with nowhere to go, and the accumulating mass releases enzymes that erode surrounding bone. The labyrinth and the facial nerve canal lie immediately adjacent to the middle ear, so erosion produces vertigo and a lower motor neuron facial palsy that involves the forehead. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has gums that are red, swollen and bleed when he brushes, with little discomfort. There is no pocketing or tooth mobility. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A dental abscess","Gingivitis, which is reversible","Oral lichen planus","Periodontitis, which causes irreversible bone loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gingivitis is the mildest form of periodontal disease: inflammation confined to the gingiva from bacterial plaque at the gum line, producing erythema, oedema and bleeding with little discomfort. The critical point is that it is entirely reversible at this stage, before the periodontal ligament and bone are destroyed. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 121"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man has recurrent painful oral ulcers. Further questioning reveals similar ulcers on the scrotum, and he has had episodes of eye inflammation and joint pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Behcet syndrome","Herpes simplex infection","Simple recurrent aphthous stomatitis","Systemic lupus erythematosus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral ulcers are the commonest feature of Behcet, affecting up to 100 per cent of patients, and genital ulcers occur in about 75 per cent and look identical to oral aphthae. The diagnosis is clinical: recurrent aphthous ulceration IN THE CONTEXT of the characteristic systemic manifestations, which here include ocular and articular involvement. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man reports constant fullness in the left ear and three months of declining hearing on that side. He had multiple middle ear infections as a child and four separate myringotomy tube placements, and he has untreated perennial airborne allergies. Weber lateralises to the left. Otoscopy shows a retracted tympanic membrane with a sac of cheesy white material, and the same debris is visible behind a small suspected perforation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Barotrauma","Cholesteatoma","Exostoses","External otitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Repeated middle ear infection, tube placements and eustachian tube dysfunction from untreated allergy all produce chronic negative middle ear pressure, which retracts the drum into a pocket. Squamous epithelium trapped in that pocket keeps shedding keratin with nowhere to go, producing the cheesy white sac described. Weber lateralising to the affected ear confirms the loss is conductive, from the mass and ossicular erosion. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has a 2 centimetre cervical node that appeared with a cold five weeks ago and has not changed in size. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Continue observation, since viral nodes are common in children","Investigate further, as it exceeds the size and duration thresholds","Reassure and discharge","Start empirical antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A node larger than 1 centimetre is abnormal, and reactive nodes should regress within one to two weeks. Persistence beyond four to six weeks, or continued enlargement, is the stated trigger for investigation, with biopsy looking for fungal, granulomatous or neoplastic causes. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has clear discharge from both nostrils, sneezing and itchy eyes each spring. The nasal mucosa is boggy with a bluish hue and the turbinates are swollen. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute bacterial sinusitis","Allergic rhinitis","Nasal foreign body","Vasomotor rhinitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral clear rhinorrhoea with a bluish, boggy, oedematous mucosa is the characteristic appearance, and the seasonal pattern with itchy eyes points to an immunoglobulin E mediated response to a pollen. The mucosal colour is the discriminating physical finding, since infective rhinitis produces erythema rather than a bluish hue. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man reports gradual reduction in hearing in the right ear over two weeks and a sensation of blockage. He uses cotton buds daily. Otoscopy shows the canal completely filled with firm wax; the drum cannot be seen. There is no pain or discharge. Which pattern of hearing loss would be expected?","choices":["A conductive loss that resolves when the wax is removed","A mixed loss requiring urgent audiology referral","A sensorineural loss from age-related cochlear change","No measurable hearing loss, since wax does not occlude sound"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Wax occluding the canal blocks air-conducted sound before it reaches the drum, while the drum, ossicles and cochlea are all normal. That is a purely mechanical conductive loss, so Weber lateralises to the blocked ear and hearing returns to baseline as soon as the obstruction is cleared. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with presbycusis is reluctant to try hearing aids because her hearing loss is not painful and she has managed so far. What should the patient be told?","choices":["Hearing aids will restore hearing to normal","The loss will eventually become painful","Untreated loss brings withdrawal and cognitive decline","Without aids the loss will progress more quickly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The consequences of age-related loss extend beyond the audiogram: reduced auditory input contributes to social isolation, depression and accelerated cognitive decline. Framing amplification as a way of protecting those things, rather than as a device for hearing better, is usually what shifts the conversation. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a solitary thyroid nodule. Her thyroid-stimulating hormone is suppressed. She is clinically mildly hyperthyroid. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Contrast computed tomography of the neck","Immediate fine needle aspiration of the nodule","Radionuclide scan with technetium before any biopsy","Start antithyroid medication and review in six months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A suppressed thyroid-stimulating hormone means the nodule may be autonomously producing hormone. A scan distinguishes a hot, hyperfunctioning nodule, which carries a low malignant risk and needs no biopsy, from a cold or warm nodule, which does. Doing the scan first avoids an unnecessary aspiration in the hot group. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 41"}]};
